INT. VICTORIAN CONSERVATORY - MORNING

Glass and iron. Ferns, orchids, a dripping fountain. A body in a gardener's apron lies under a toppled palm in a spreading pool of water. Fresh footprints cross the tile.

INSPECTOR HALVERSON (50s, rumpled tweed) crouches by the corpse. Beside him, SERGEANT PEMBERTON (30s, notebook, damp umbrella) stands guard. In wicker chairs near the door sit MISS CORDELIA FITCH (60s, the owner, pearls and a cane) and MR. ASHWORTH THE BOTANIST (50s, glasses, ink-stained cuffs).

HALVERSON
Who found him?

MISS FITCH
I did. I came for my tea.

MR. ASHWORTH
She screamed. I came running.

HALVERSON
Did either of you touch him?

MISS FITCH
Certainly not.

MR. ASHWORTH
I only checked the pulse.

HALVERSON
Where were you at six?

MISS FITCH
In bed.

MR. ASHWORTH
In the library.

PEMBERTON
(to Halverson, low)
She lied. The housemaid said she was awake at five.

HALVERSON
(to himself)
Interesting.

He rises. Walks the tiled path slowly. Stops at the fountain. Peers into the water. He picks something out with two fingers: a silver button.

HALVERSON (CONT'D)
(holding it up)
Whose is this?

MISS FITCH
Not mine.

MR. ASHWORTH
Not mine.

Silence. The fountain gurgles. Pemberton, flipping pages, mutters a name. Halverson glances at him.

HALVERSON
Say that again, Sergeant.

PEMBERTON
The victim's coat had one button missing.

MISS FITCH
He had it sewn.

MR. ASHWORTH
He had it sewn last Tuesday.

HALVERSON
Then this one is not his.

He looks at both of them in turn. Miss Fitch's hand tightens on the cane. Mr. Ashworth's mouth opens, then shuts.

HALVERSON (CONT'D)
(to everyone)
Nobody leaves the glasshouse.

Overhead, a pane of glass rattles in the wind.
